92186083662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083359 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083359 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083359 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to Inverse Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083443 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "66462250-9bf6-5b79-837b-72d46f60efbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084318 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084318 #edb/ref 1061 41715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f836d465-3cfd-57b6-ab05-79619fabc9a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084319 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084319 #edb/ref 1061 41716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084319 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e3f4334-5c00-58e4-9032-fed944bab672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084319 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084320 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084320 #edb/ref 1061 41719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084320 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d19f2942-df0f-5896-adf0-8c5b3ca192b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084320 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084321 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084321 #edb/ref 1061 41722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084321 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "26073589-e276-519e-b495-acab562e4cb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084321 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084322 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084322 #edb/ref 1061 41725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084322 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3130ad01-93cf-56c2-a59d-52d2324277cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084322 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084323 #edb/ref 1061 41726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084323 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84556b63-2621-5c1b-84d0-55dbf5fff5d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084323 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084499 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the mapping diagram of the function $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/41/415b9ce71454f2aec9a2fc678c79f1d34fbb16468fbac50fc62b76283b9c209a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's now reverse the arrows, as follows:\n\n![](images/80/801b3eca4820b110c2145bd13fbf1faddbb0db83905869410cad1dc8a32f4e83.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we have a mapping diagram that \"reverses\" the action of the function $f.$ The diagram itself represents a new function, which we call the **inverse of the function $f,$** and denote by $f^{-1}.$\n\nWe can evaluate the inverse function $f^{-1}$ at any point in the range of $f.$ For example, to find the value of $f^{-1}(2),$ we notice that we have an arrow from ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right) to ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left). Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}({\\color{black}2}) = {\\color{black}0}.\n$$\n\nIn the original function, $f,$ that same arrow went from ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left) to ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right). So, we have that $f({\\color{black}0})={\\color{black}2},$  and as a result, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(0)) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the function $f$ and then \"reversing\" the action using $f^{-1}$ will get us back to $0.$\n\nIn general, this is true for every $x$ in the domain of the function $f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084499 #edb/ref 1065 6883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084499 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "05fd6236-8b7b-59d7-bf60-5e7d9d2db65a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084499 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084500 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) \"reverses\" the action of ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) so that\n![](images/ea/eab0458598a0b4298ccbfcd1664babf085309c0017ba0e530c76900f38bb8678.svg)\nwhich can be visualized using the mapping diagram, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3dd33f3f90eaadec2f1168db89665d1986e562005fc01cd9f58dbc3f17e7a616.png)\n\n \n\nSince ![](images/aa/aabc5ff96d4807ff058c8f3e9f78aa70ced5e624ac2ae1ebdd1bb4597d6c6dad.svg) is just the composition ![](images/5a/5aeac36928f2e7a97b7c71c2c97dd9bef3774970b3efc871c9131c6d2075be5e.svg) we can now give the algebraic definition of the inverse. \n\nThe function ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) is called the **inverse of the function ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg)** if\n![](images/52/52a4e32d6165988fe50c0766a1310fd3afb895f4a11149ff7b45c1cb0dd76e3a.svg)\nfor each ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) in the domain of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg)\n\nIn other words, to show that two functions ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) and ![](images/a9/a9e111e418e432fd154a819bb521c373ca64e9b27bbeed17a5777bba6940c6b6.svg) are inverses of each other, we need to check the following condition:\n![](images/7f/7facf5a55f1e90f9b9aac2358e4bc4b0560c2bd4b9fa2700e902a4dde84f90b7.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084500 #edb/ref 1065 6884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084500 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f4a2e52b-5aee-58ed-bcc3-b236246d862c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084500 #edb/ref 1067 "The Definition of the Inverse Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124564 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124564 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124564 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124564 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124564 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124564 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Global Minimum of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124564 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124564 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124624 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124624 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124624 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124624 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186507446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124624 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Global Maximum of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124624 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124624 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "86517de9-0e7c-5c00-a3e8-7aeccbf12d9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124625 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124625 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124625 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124625 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Whether the Global Extrema of a Function Exist" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124625 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124625 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d9f1c88f-1c8a-52f9-a144-b52f67564f5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1045 "Global Extrema of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124693 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "dd6d8fc7-ed4f-5412-9ac7-72ddd6e6fc3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125475 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125475 #edb/ref 1061 24350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125475 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f190feb-818e-5e2a-8ed1-9a841eb19c11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125475 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125581 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125581 #edb/ref 1061 3625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125581 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a61d8ec6-2820-5303-a832-f51a73657cc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125581 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125582 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125582 #edb/ref 1061 3626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ce76583f-0ff9-522f-8324-5c6a6492916a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125583 #edb/ref 1061 3629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125583 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a936946d-a2c6-5c5b-8e3e-ad8903a06ff2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125583 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125713 #edb/ref 1064 "When we deal with functions, we are often interested in their largest or smallest values.\n\n- The **global maximum** of a function is the largest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum. It's also called the **absolute maximum.**\n\n- The **global minimum** of a function is the smallest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum. It's also called the **absolute minimum.**\n\nIf one or both exist, the global maximum and minimum values of a function are collectively called the **global extrema** of the function. \n\nFor example, in the graph below, the global maximum is $2$, while the global minimum is $-2$.\n\n![](images/22/2235cefbb995edcc085f96d8a5ff4e1ddd83f6345091c90ec3e0a7797fb9d8f3.png)\n\nThe point where a function reaches its global maximum/minimum is called the **global maximum/minimum point**.  So in this case, \n\n- $(-2,2)$ is the global maximum point, and\n\n- $(1,-2)$ is the global minimum point.\n\n**Watch out!** The *global maximum/minimum* is unique. It's a value of the function ($y$-coordinate on the graph). However, there can be several *global maximum/minimum points*. All of these will have the same $y$-coordinate (*global maximum/minimum*) but different $x$-coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125713 #edb/ref 1065 1495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125713 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cfedbc54-8dd6-532f-9075-109e336b43a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125713 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129106 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129106 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129106 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Roots of a Function Given Its Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129106 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129106 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "82f5682f-73b8-5c0a-b7d3-3336200843a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129107 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129107 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129107 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129107 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Roots of a Function Given a Table" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129107 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129107 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "02e29602-b3c0-5ceb-ab65-710c06a08c1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129108 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129108 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129108 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129108 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129108 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Roots of a Function Given a Mapp